Subject: SOCI: PART 2 of 2 of the "Brave New World" article

	Here is PART 2 of 2 of the "Brave New World" article.  It is shorter
than part 1.  :)

Mark Venture
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

	And what will life be like for the problematic fetuses not aborted?
If there are fewer people in need of special services, will there be fewer
special services?

	The economic reality of a universal health-care system might well
cost Americans some of their rights to conception and reproduction.  The
day could come, for instance, when genetic screening would be mandated
for all Medicare mothers.

	"Maybe we'll be told we can't have a child with cystic fibrosis
or Huntington's disease," says Ted Peters, a theologian at the Center
for Theology and the Natural Sciences at the Graduate Theological Union
in Berkley, Calif.  The interdenominational center is halfway through
a three-year grant to study the theological, ethical and philosophical
problems posed by the Genome Project.

	"One of our objectives is to study how we can inform the clergy
about these issues because a lot of people struggling with them are
going to end up in the pastor's office," he says.

	Peters is not surprised that most parents who know they are
carrying a defective fetus opt for abortion.  "I think that's the
human thing to do, which is why leaders of state, religious leaders
and others need to constantly hold society up to the higher ideal
that God loves each one of us, regardless of our deficiences."

	But not since Darwin's challenge to the biblical story of
creation has religion faced such doctrinal provocation.  Many
denominations proved flexible then, deciding that the Bible's
version of creation was metaphor, not to be taken literally.

	[Interesting memetic footwork.  -MV]

	"Should we manipulate genes in terms of our own moral ideals,
such as relieving suffering?  Or is suffering not such a bad thing in
God's eyes?" asks Peters.

	If humans are determined by their genes, is free will an illusion?
How then should "sin" be determined?  If homosexuality is proved to be
genetic, should all religions accept gays and fight for their rights?

	Abortion probably will remain a black-white issue for those
adamantly opposed to it, despite advances in genetic technology.

	"The Catholic Church supports only genetic diagnosis or genetic
interventions that are therapeutic for the fetus," says Shannon.  "The
problem is, our diagnostic capacity outstrips our therapeutic intervention.
We don't understand what all of the findings mean.  We can identify classic
Down's syndrome, but we can't tell what the range (of severity) is."

	Spiraling health care costs will only fuel the abortion wars and
probably edge out morality issues in some quarters.  Americans like to
think of themselves as a moral people, but already "a real social
intolerance is growing up around having children who could have been
aborted, because they are expensive to take care of," Shannon says.

	Karen Lebacqz, an ethicist at the Pacific School of Religion, an
interdenominational seminary in Berkley, Calif., agrees.  "There's already
evidence that women who choose not to undergo amniocentesis are asked
questions like: 'Why don't you?  Do you have religious objections?'  As if
you are either stupid or religious for not having the tests."

	If it is proved that much of who we become is fixed in our genes,
then American cultural assumptions as basic as equal opportunity will be
severely challenged.  People who want to show that certain groups don't
have the same intellectual, emotional or psychological potential will use
chromosomal analysis to make their point.

	"It will make the debate about whether IQ is genetic look like a
holiday at the beach," says Caplan.

	Indeed, the day may come when old-fashioned genetic ignorance is
to be looked upon as a kind of bliss.

	"Before, there was a given.  We had to live with people we didn't
necessarily choose," says Lysaught, of the Chicago ethics center.  "Were
we better off?  What are the benefits we're losing here?"

	As the theologians like to say, God only knows.

End PART 2 of 2


