Date: Mon, 15 Mar 1993 17:54:10 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: reason

Stephen Withrow writes an excellent article on assigning values to
beliefs.

> the ideal world-view would perform well in each of these
> measures. 
 
> 1) It must not conflict with the empirical evidence, and elements of the
> hypothesis (or grand unified theory) must be consistent with each other.

I agree this is also one of my ideal characteristics for a belief,
that it be true, but I note that sometimes this conflicts with
anti-entropic outcomes.  At least two varieties: (1) functional
delusions, in which irrational beliefs and irrational phenotypes combine
to create rational behaviors, and (2) false but simple beliefs 
producing behavior that could also be derived from true but
complex beliefs; the irrational belief is more efficient and may
require less information from the environment.

> 2) It should motivate the individual to carry out strategies and behave in
> ways which are to his / her personal self-interest

One problem with this is, there is no rational, measurable,
non-circular definition of "self-interest".

> 3) It should optimise the subjective mental states and self-esteem of the
> individual.

This again provides no basis for measurement.  Can you demonstrate 
that death does not constitute the optimal mental state?  To make 
such a claim, you have to fall back on another set of beliefs, eg that
death is bad.  Why is that belief valuable?  Etc.

The fundamental problem with these criterion are, how are they tied
to our goal of anti-entropy?  For example from this basis we could
conclude that death increases entropy, and therefore devalue it
as well as the beliefs, motivations, etc. that tend to promote it.
I suggest we start by creating measurable anti-entropic benchmarks,
and work out the valuation of beliefs from there, instead of
starting with unmeasurable, at best vaguely defined concepts like
"self-interest" and "self-esteem".

> Since individuals are not the same, I don't believe that the optimum
> world-view for one individual is necessarily the best for another --

This is likely true, and thus we should always keep in mind the
value of diversity and not jump to conclusions that some belief
is "entropic" when we have not in fact demonstrated it to be so.
Nevertheless, it should be possible with the right toolbox to
critique beliefs, motvations, etc. for their entropic consequences,
and on the other hand design beliefs and motivations that maximize
extropy.

Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

