Robin Hanson makes a concrete proposal:

>   "An armed society is a polite society" - Anon

Depending on the final form, I may join.

> Of course anyone is free to create other societies
> intended to serve similar functions.  I may even join them.

OK, as a supporter of memetic competition and not believing
that there's very much motivation to join a society where arbitrary
lurkers can snipe from the stands without commitment or real loss,
I do so: "The Accountability Society".  In THIS society, any member
may force any other member to back up his or her positions.  It works
like this:  If I as a member think that some other member has posted
something that is complete nonsense or otherwise insulting, or
for WHATEVER REASON, I can CHALLENGE that person by asking a SPECIFIC
question about his or her statement (posting).  The CHALLENGED member
must then respond to the question within one week with a reasoned
essay of at least 800 words answering the question.  The CHALLENGER
must then respond, within a week, to that essay with an 800 word essay
of his or her own, on the topic.

As with Robin's society, membership in this one only takes effect
two weeks after declaration.  Declaration is to be a public 
notice of the following form:

  I, XXX, am willing to back up my statements.  For this reason,
  I am henceforward a member of The Accountability Society.

Also as with Robin's Politeness Society, this is independent
of formal list management.

Upon receiving a CHALLENGE, a member may decline if the
challenge is not relevant to the post being challenged.  To
do so, simply write a message to Extropians clearly stating
this, with adequate justification.  Also, if a member has
in the past ignored a CHALLENGE or failed to fulfill the
requirements of a CHALLENGER, statement of such reasons will
be adequate excusse for ignoring the CHALLENGE.

This is largely a reputation-based society, with admirable
self-balancing qualities; that is, it is possible to reply
to a CHALLENGE with eight hundred words of gibberish, or
altogether ignore the rules, but only if one is willing to
look like a complete imbecile.  After carefully examining
the abuses that can take place, I think you'll agree that
they hurt the abuser more than the abused.

Besides reducing hit-and-run junk mail, this society should
inject a welcome continuity of reasoned debate.

So dedicate your keyboard to your principles!  Be willing to
support your views!  Join The Accountability Society!  It's
the right thing to do!

derek expansion libertario III

for the record (despite the experimental name, quite serious):

  I, Derek Expansion Libertario III, am willing to back up my
  statements.  For this reason, I am henceforward a member of
  The Accountability Society.

