Here's an experience that somewhat astonished me, and perhaps might be 
useful:

During the morning if I don't have to go in to work, I often lie in a 
half-sleep state that is accompanied by ruminating, [day]dreaming, and 
envisioning various scenarios.  A few mornings ago, having been on the 
net for several hours the previous evening, I started ruminating 
over a few posts I had read (as is typically of this state, they 
were sort of randomly mushed together).  While thinking about 
what I wanted to type in response, I start envisioning myself typing, 
and the computer screen with text appeared in my mind.  It became quite 
vivid, so that I could recognize the font.  Curiously, the font had 
holes chopped in it, like it was moth-eaten.  I found that if I tried 
to read one or two of the words, the others on the "screen" would 
wink out.  Then a new set of words would reappear, a random but seemingly
grammatical jumble of words, as if Racter had been run over the e-mail
I had been pondering.  I was able to consciously go through several
cycles of this.  I tried "typing" both by voicing words in my mind & 
moving my fingers but that didn't seem to work.  I tried doing various
"pasting" operations like pasting graphics, but without much luck; 
after some mucking around like that the whole "scene" faded.

(No drugs, BTW, unless it was the "Deep Thought" vitamin & amino
acid formula I took the previous day).

Anybody else dream in computer screens?  Is it possible to
improve on this "skill"?  I wonder if this has any relationship
to "photographic memory" (which I've never experienced in detail).
I speculate about training until I can use my half-dream state
as a sort of word processor, or even do it while awake and calm,
eg while studying in a library.  Of course, I don't know how I'll
save the "files", and I'd still have to dump them to a real computer
thru the fingers, but it'd beat shelling out $1,000's for a 
portable. :-)

