Alas, I don't think it's in my best interest to join the
Feuding Society.  (It's better to name it by its direct
effect than by its alleged indirect effect).  Folks will shoot
not only when a person is impolite, but also when they disagree 
emotionally with them, or feel threatened by their beliefs.  For 
example if this was implemented on sci.space, NASA readers and astronaut 
fans would have shot me off the forum years ago, because I'm one
of the few posters to frequently post significant criticisms of
their strategy. I frequently get "shot" in the form of gratuitous
flames & insults, which may hurt my reputation but doesn't stop
me from posting.   With a Feuding Society sci.space would turn
into a NASA house of worship, patrolled by its taxpayer-funded
goons.

On this forum my disagreements are much less, but I still tend to post 
on the points that need work rather than on the things most of us 
already agree on, and I'm better at stating facts & opinions than at 
empathy & patience. As a frequent poster I'm at a major disadvantage 
to the lurkers under some rulesets. As for the overall extropy, the 
Feuding Society like many of the ratings proposals favors the popular 
post at the expense of the true post.  

It might be fun to see other folks shooting each other
to pieces.  A negative-sum game. :-)

Derek Zahn's Accountability Society, where challenges lead to
volleys of 800-word essays, may be useful. It might
be better to restrict challenges to questions of fact (eg point out
3 facts, with references, that support your position) or proof
(eg post a logical proof that X is never true), in other words make
it very specific and logical and judgeable, but not have a wordcount 
requirement.

