The 1994 International Conference on Communications (ICC'94) will be
held in New Orleans during the first week of May, 1994.  ICC is one
of the two major international conferences on research and
development in telecommunications sponsored annually by the
IEEE Communications Society.  ICC'94 will present about 50 technical
sessions over three days.  An attendance of about 2000 is typical
for ICC.

The general Call for Papers for ICC'94 has just been published in the
March, 1993, issue of the IEEE Communications Magazine.  Attached below
is a call for papers specifically for a proposed session at ICC'94 on
wireless multimedia systems, a topic that is already of great interest.
All papers submitted to ICC'94 will be reviewed.  Complete manuscripts
of submitted papers, generally limited to 3000 words, will be due by
31 August 1993 to the ICC'94 Technical Program Committee.

If you are interested in submitting a paper for this session,
please contact either of the following for more information and
a copy of the general ICC'94 Call for Papers:

   Jeff H. Derby                        Ramin A. Nobakht
   IBM Corporation, RTP, NC, USA        IBM Corporation, RTP, NC, USA
   (919) 254-3446 (voice)               (919) 543-6569 (voice)
   (919) 543-7575 (fax)                 (919) 543-7575 (fax)
   jhderby@vnet.ibm.com (e-mail)        ran@ralvm29.vnet.ibm.com (e-mail)

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   Call for Papers:  ICC'94 session on 'Wireless Multimedia Systems'

Personal mobile communication systems (PCS) are widely recognized as the
wave of the future in telecommunications.  A common goal of all these
systems is to provide communications services to users, independent of
the users' current locations and in a reliable, timely, and
cost-effective manner.  With the increasing popularity of laptop and
palmtop computers, users will expect PCS to support the full suite of
communication services available to them at their office workstations,
including those needed for multimedia applications.  Capabilities of
interest include digital voice, data, facsimile, image, and eventually,
support for full-motion video and videoconferencing.

The technical questions associated with PCS support for multimedia
applications are many.  Choices must be made among several modulation
methods, error-control approaches, multiple access schemes, and coders
and compression algorithms for speech and image.  The session we are
proposing is intended to focus on the issues associated with designing,
building, and managing mobile communications systems that support
multimedia.  Topics include but are not limited to the following:

        - Modulation and coding methods
        - Error control coding approaches
        - Speech coding schemes
        - Image/Video coding schemes
        - Equalization methods
        - Viterbi decoding approaches
        - Multi-media data integration schemes
        - Advanced mobile services

We seek papers that provide information in one or more of the
following forms: presentation of recent research results, description
of experimental results gained through implementation or simulation
experience, comparison of alternative approaches, discussion of open
issues, and tutorials.



